## Installing and launching IMASViz from sources

### Requirements

IMASViz walks the data dictionary through the Access Layer metadata of each IDS, so it
needs the python HLI hosted at https://github.com/iterorganization/IMAS-Python.git
(the IMAS-Python module, distribution "imas-python").

IMASViz CANNOT run on the generated python HLI hosted at
ssh://git@git.iter.org/imas/al-python.git (the IMAS-AL-Python module, distribution that pack
"imas"). IMAS-AL-Python is deprecated and carries no metadata reflection API at all. 

IMASViz requires the following libraries:

- IMAS (the IMAS Python HLI, provided by the IMAS-Python module)

- Python3.x and the following Python libraries:
   - PySide6 (Qt for Python — the sources import PySide6, not PyQt5)
   - pyqtgraph
   - matplotlib

   - Sphinx
   (pip3 install sphinx)
   No Sphinx theme is needed: the manual is built on Sphinx's own ``basic``
   theme and dressed by ``doc/source/_static/imasviz.css``, which is in the
   repository. (It used to ask for ``sphinx_rtd_theme``, which is not installed
   on the nodes IMASViz is built on -- ``make doc`` stopped there.)


### Obtaining the source code

Clone the IMASViz repository from **https://github.com/IRFM/IMASViz.git**.

By default, the main branch will be created.

To switch to the develop branch:

    git checkout develop

### Setting the environment

To set the environment, load the modules providing IMAS-Core, IMAS-Python, PySide6, pyqtgraph,
and matplotlib, then go to the **repository root** (the top-level package
is **imasviz/**) and set **VIZ_HOME**, **VIZ_PRODUCTION**,
**IMAS_VIZ_VERSION** and **UDA_DISABLED** environment variables.

On the Eurofusion Gateway (EFGW), execute the following commands:

    module load ach-modules
    module load IMAS-Core/5.7.1-intel-2023b
    module load PySide6/6.6.2-GCCcore-13.2.0
    module load IMAS-Python/2.3.0-intel-2023b
    module load PyQtGraph/0.13.7-intel-2023b

    cd IMASViz

    # bash
    export VIZ_PRODUCTION=1
    export VIZ_HOME=$PWD
    export IMAS_VIZ_VERSION=3.0.0
    export UDA_DISABLED=1 #disable UDA

    # csh
    setenv VIZ_PRODUCTION 1
    setenv VIZ_HOME $PWD
    setenv IMAS_VIZ_VERSION=3.0.0
    setenv UDA_DISABLED 1 #disable UDA

On EFGW nodes the foss variant fails to load that
requirement, and what runs is the **intel** HLI instead —
`IMAS-Python/2.3.0-intel-2023b`, `PyQtGraph/0.13.7-intel-2023b`. See [Starting
on the Gateway](#starting-from-sources-gateway-iter-hpc-west): the scripts in `scripts/` load that
stack and set this environment for you.

on ITER cluster, execute the following commands:

    module load IMAS-Core
    module load IMAS-Python
    module load PyQtGraph
    module load matplotlib


IMASViz reads the IMAS Data Dictionary at run time through the Access Layer
Python bindings (imas.ids_metadata), so no parser code is generated any more.
No dictionary file is needed either: the shot view asks the case it has opened
which data dictionary version that case is stored with (DBEntry.dd_version) and
takes the list of IDSs and their documentation from the factory of that version
(imas.IDSFactory), which the IMAS-Python distribution carries inside its own
package. A data entry (case) is opened **at its own version**: an entry left unpinned settles
on the newest dictionary the package holds (4.1.1 on the stack above), and the
Access Layer then reads every IDS of a case written under another major version
while still listing its contents -- so IMASViz reads the version the URI spells
(version=3, or the <db>/<data version>/<shot>/<run> of a path case), asks the
case the exact version its first filled IDS names, and opens the entry there.
**IMAS_VERSION**, if you export it, is the version of the data
dictionary — IMAS-Python reads that variable itself. 

The repository Makefile builds the documentation only:

        # Main command (documentation)
        make

### Configuring UDA

1. The IMAS access layer used by IMASViz should support UDA for accessing remote UDA servers.

2. The file 'UDA_machines' located in directory $VIZ_HOME/config lists all tokamaks with remote UDA servers which
can be accessed from the current machine where IMASViz is installed.
Please refers to the documentation for the required prerequisites for accessing a particular remote UDA server.


### Running IMASViz from sources

The modules loaded in **Setting the environment** already provide the IMAS
Python HLI, PySide6, pyqtgraph and matplotlib, so no additional module load is
required. A graphical display (X) is needed to show the window:

    python $VIZ_HOME/imasviz/VizGUI/QtVIZ_GUI.py

### Installing and browsing IMASViz documentation

**IMASViz documentation** can be generated to multiple formats, e.g. **HTML**, **PDF**...

In $VIZ_HOME directory execute:

    make doc

    # to open PDF documentation
    xdg-open doc/build/latex/IMASViz.pdf

    # to open HTML documentation
    firefox doc/build/html/index.html



### Saving and restoring a session

**File -> Save session** or **File -> Load session** (from the application window's *File* menu) allows saving and restoring what is on the screen — the cases that are open, the IDS occurrences they have loaded and the time slices each was asked for, the selection, the plot windows with the key the Windows menu and its Delete lines address them by, and the windows a plugin drew — to **$HOME/.imasviz/<name>.vsess**. 

**File -> Load session** lists those files and puts one back: the cases are reopened and the windows drawn again by the same commands that drew them the first time, so a restored window owns its sliders, its axis and its legend. What had been *set* on a window comes back with it: where its time sliders stood, which of the error lines were on and at what step, the range a plot figure was zoomed onto, and where its window sat in the area — maximized or hidden, hidden not closed. A plugin window comes back by the same rule: the file names which plugin, which entry of it, on which IDS and at which occurrence, and the restore asks that entry again with those answers, so the user is asked nothing — the window returns as its entry draws it, which means a tab added by hand is gone. See the manual, *Other GUI features -> Save/Load Session*, for what the file keeps and what it does not keep.

Closing IMASViz writes the session that was on screen to
**$HOME/.imasviz/last_session.vsess**, and the next start asks once whether to
put it back — a question that is not asked at all when no such file is there.

The IDSs of a session are read lazily through IMAS-Python, which is what makes
putting a session back affordable: on the reference case, building the tree of
one *equilibrium* occurrence costs 0,10 s and 7,7 Mo this way against 3,99 s and
122,8 Mo read whole, with the same tree either way.

## Driving IMASViz from an agent (MCP tools)

IMASViz can be driven by an MCP agent the way it is driven by its menus:
`mcp_server/` exposes the gestures of one application session as tools of the
official `mcp` SDK (v1 `FastMCP`), over `streamable_http` on a local socket.
The application stays the main program — the GUI runs its event loop and is
visible; the transport is one daemon thread, and every tool is executed on the
GUI thread through `mcp_server/dispatcher.py`, whatever thread the request
came from.

### Requirements

The same stack as the application, plus the SDK (v1 — the 2.x line renamed the
surface). On the ACH nodes whose default profile pins
`SciPy-bundle/2023.12-iimkl`, the HLI is the **intel** variant (the foss one
fails to load its SciPy-bundle requirement there; verified 2026-11-05):

    module load PySide6/6.6.2-GCCcore-13.2.0
    module load IMAS-Python/2.3.0-intel-2023b
    module load PyQtGraph/0.13.7-intel-2023b
    pip install --user "mcp[http]<2"     # FastMCP + starlette + uvicorn

### Starting the session with its tools

From the repository root, with `VIZ_HOME` and the environment as above:

    python -m mcp_server --port 8765          # the GUI comes up and is visible
    python -m mcp_server --offscreen          # a node without a display

and any MCP client pointed at the endpoint. In Qwen Code, the entry goes in
the `mcpServers` of `settings.json`, on the `httpUrl` field (the streamable
transport; `url` is the SSE one), and the server must be running before the
session starts — Qwen Code loads its MCP servers at startup:

    { "mcpServers": { "imasviz": {
          "httpUrl": "http://127.0.0.1:8765/mcp",
          "timeout": 600000,
          "discoveryTimeoutMs": 15000 } } }

What a tool answers is the answer of the application: a refused gesture
returns the named `ValueError` the command raises (as an error of the
call), and `get_logs(uri)` holds those refusals the way the log panel holds
them for the user.

### The tools

| Group | Tools |
| --- | --- |
| cases | `open_case`, `list_cases`, `close_case` |
| tree | `list_idss`, `load_ids`, `search_nodes`, `select_signals`, `unselect_all`, `list_selection`, `select_from_configuration` |
| plotting | `plot_one`, `plot_selection`, `plot_table`, `plot_stacked`, `get_signal`, `save_plot_configuration` |
| windows | `list_windows`, `show_window`, `hide_window`, `close_window` |
| plugins | `list_plugins`, `run_plugin` |
| session | `save_session`, `restore_session`, `export_to_ids`, `list_sessions`, `list_configurations`, `save_selection` |
| inspection | `get_screenshot` (PNG of a window), `get_logs` (the case log panel), `describe_node` |
| reports | `plot_report` (one window of curves as self-contained HTML with stats), `case_report` (the whole loaded tree as HTML with stats) |

Stylage and cursors are kept in the hands of the user; they are not offered
as tools. The suite drives this layer in `tests/test_100_mcp_core_offscreen.py`
(without the SDK) and `tests/test_101_mcp_tools_http.py` (over a real
transport).

### The terminal client (viz_cli)

The same gestures, from the shell, without a window: `viz_cli` is the in-app
agent on your own keyboard. It holds **one** agent session open for its whole
life — the offscreen application, its core, and one `AgentLoop` — so a prompt
can point at what the one before it named. The arrow keys, the tab-completion
and the history are the client's own (this node's Python has no C module behind
`readline`), and the history file is the one it keeps in
`$HOME/.imasviz/cli_history`, between runs, as the preferences are kept.

    python3 -m viz_cli                          # the REPL, offscreen
    python3 -m viz_cli "trace le champ"         # one prompt, then exit (0 if it
                                                # answered, 1 if it refused)

Inside the REPL, a line runs the agent; the `/` commands steer it:

- `/help` — the commands and the gestures this instance offers;
- `/llm <field> <value>` — the model it runs against, read from and written to
  the **same** `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences` the GUI reads (`AgentLLMEndpoint`,
  `AgentLLMModel`, `AgentLLMApiKey`); `/llm` alone reads them back.
  `max_turns`, `temperature` and `max_tokens` live on the live client, for that
  run;
- `/mcp [name]` — the tools this session exposes and, for one, how to call it
  (e.g. `/mcp plot_selection`);
- `/session` — the cases open in this session, or `/session save <name>` and
  `/session restore <name>` to write and put one back;
- `/shots [dir]` — where the turns' images and transcripts are saved;
- `/history` — the prompts of this client run;
- `/quit` — leave (the arrows keep your prompts for the next run).

What a turn answers is the answer of the application the way `test_100` pins
it: a refused gesture is named, and `get_logs` holds those refusals. On a node
without a display the client is offscreen by design; `--display` keeps the
window when you have one.

### The web interface (viz_web)

The same gestures, from a browser — and the IMASViz window stays offscreen:
a node without a display (a tablet) drives the whole session through one
self-contained page, `viz_web/static/index.html`, no build step and no new
dependency (it is served by the `starlette` + `uvicorn` the MCP server
already required).

    python3 -m viz_web                          # on http://127.0.0.1:8770
    python3 -m viz_web --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8771   # another device on the box

`--offscreen` forces the offscreen platform even when a `DISPLAY` is set (it
is the default when there is none); `--llm-url` / `--llm-model` /
`--llm-key` name the local model for the run, otherwise the `AgentLLM*`
lines of `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences` stand, the same file the REPL and the
in-app agent read. A device on the network points its browser at the box's
address and port.

The page is the REPL with a picture. The first column runs the agent: a
prompt, and the transcript streamed in the order the loop writes it —
refusals included, in the log colour they wear in the panel. The second
column lists the raw gestures, one form per tool with its parameters, run
the way the loop runs them (so `plot_one` given an argument it does not
take is refused by name, the names it does hold beside it). Every turn
leaves in `~/.imasviz/web/shots/<n>/` its transcript and the PNG of the
application and of each of its windows, which the third column re-draws
under *Résultats*, and the *Turns* list of the first column names the
turns the session has had. The model's running parameters are read from
and written to the same preferences through the page's own `/llm` line.

The suite drives it in `tests/test_119_viz_web_session.py` (one turn at a
time, the refusals named, the context between turns),
`tests/test_120_viz_web_routes.py` (the routes, the stream, the shots read
back) and `tests/test_121_viz_web_house_style.py` (the page wears the
application's colours in both themes, and nothing of it imports a web
framework this node does not hold).

## Starting from sources (Gateway, ITER HPC, WEST)

Each site has its own launcher pair in `scripts/` — one for the **visible
application** (needs X) and one for the **terminal client** (offscreen, or
with `--display`). All use `VIZ_PRODUCTION=1` and locate `VIZ_HOME` from the
script's own path, so they can be run from anywhere. `module` is a shell
function and is never piped — a pipe would run the load in a subshell and lose
the environment it sets.

### Gateway (EFGW)

Loads the **intel** HLI (the foss variant does not load its SciPy-bundle
requirement here).

    scripts/run_imasviz.sh                      # the application, with its window
    scripts/run_viz_cli.sh                      # the terminal client
    scripts/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"         # one-shot, exit 0/1

### ITER HPC

Loads the four standard modules (no version pin, the profile resolves the
active stack).

    scripts/iter/run_imasviz.sh                 # the application, with its window
    scripts/iter/run_viz_cli.sh                 # the terminal client
    scripts/iter/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"    # one-shot

### WEST

Creates a Python virtualenv at `~/.imasviz/venv` on first run, pip-installs
`IMAS-Python`, `PySide6`, `PyQtGraph` and `matplotlib` into it (the stack is
re-verified on every run), then loads the IMAS core with `module load IMAS`.

    scripts/west/run_imasviz.sh                 # the application, with its window
    scripts/west/run_viz_cli.sh                 # the terminal client
    scripts/west/run_viz_cli.sh "one prompt"    # one-shot

All six launchers set, for the production run: `VIZ_HOME`, `VIZ_PRODUCTION=1`,
`UDA_DISABLED=1`, `TS_MAPPINGS_DIR` (default `$repo/ts_mapping_files`) and
`IMAS_VIZ_VERSION` (overridable from the environment). They do **not** replace
the modules' `PYTHONPATH`; the code appends `VIZ_HOME` to `sys.path` itself.
`run_viz_cli.sh` hands any argument straight to `viz_cli`, so it is the one to
start a one-shot prompt from the shell.

The web interface keeps no site launcher of its own: it starts with
`python3 -m viz_web` once the same loading above holds, on the one condition
that `starlette` and `uvicorn` import (on Gateway, the MCP requirement's
`pip install --user "mcp[http]<2"` already brought them; elsewhere they come
from the same line, or from the site's virtualenv).

## The in-app agent

IMASViz can run the agent itself: the application talks to a local LLM
(`llama-server` by default, `127.0.0.1:8080`) and drives its own gestures
through the same `McpGuiCore` the MCP surface uses. No external agent or
MCP client is needed in production.

The agent is visible in the *Windows* menu as `Agent:1`. Type a prompt,
click *Tourner*, and the LLM chooses the gestures — the transcript shows
each step, and refusals are named the same way they appear in the log panel.

Configuration (in `$HOME/.imasviz/preferences`):

    AgentLLMEndpoint=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1
    AgentLLMModel=                          # empty = read from /v1/models
    AgentLLMApiKey=                         # empty = no auth header

The suite drives the agent loop and panel in `tests/test_110_agent_loop.py`
(scripted LLM, no network) and `tests/test_111_agent_panel.py` (widget,
registered in `figureframes`).
